{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_20","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md | sed -n '635,770p'; nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.divergence-register.md | sed -n '308,380p'; nl -ba src/cljc/yin/repl/core.cljc | sed -n '430,487p'; nl -ba src/cljc/yin/repl/core.cljc | sed -n '535,580p'; sed -n '180,225p' src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc; rg -n 'restore-initial-env|tailcall|:return|20 ' src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_20","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md | sed -n '635,770p'; nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.divergence-register.md | sed -n '308,380p'; nl -ba src/cljc/yin/repl/core.cljc | sed -n '430,487p'; nl -ba src/cljc/yin/repl/core.cljc | sed -n '535,580p'; sed -n '180,225p' src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc; rg -n 'restore-initial-env|tailcall|:return|20 ' src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc\"","aggregated_output":"   635\tenv `merge`; both have reserved optimizations (§6.4) that require no datom\n   636\tchange.\n   637\t\n   638\t### 6.3 Cache locality\n   639\t\n   640\tThe image is one contiguous array of small decoded records built in pc\n   641\torder, so sequential execution walks memory sequentially; a loop body is a\n   642\tfew cache lines revisited. Walker nodes are wherever the reader or\n   643\t`datoms->ast` allocated them, and each continuation frame is a fresh\n   644\tallocation on the nursery. On V8 and Dart, where keyword `case` compiles to\n   645\thash-and-compare chains and small maps are dictionary-mode objects, the\n   646\tinteger switch and array indexing matter more than on HotSpot; the historic\n   647\tbenchmarks put semantic bytecode at 2–7x slower on those hosts precisely\n   648\tbecause it did graph traversal and map lookups per step, both of which this\n   649\tdesign removes.\n   650\t\n   651\t### 6.4 Relation to bytecode, and what is reserved\n   652\t\n   653\tThe loaded image *is* bytecode. The difference from a traditional bytecode\n   654\tVM is only the stored form (five datoms per instruction rather than a few\n   655\tbytes) and the load step that decodes it. Execution cost is therefore\n   656\tbytecode-class by construction, and queryability is not paid for on the hot\n   657\tpath at all — it is paid once at load. The historic 1.0–1.1x register/stack\n   658\tline in `cesk-space-optimization.md` is the target; the walker sits at\n   659\t1.32x on that workload, the old graph-walking semantic VM at 1.69x.\n   660\t\n   661\tReserved, additive, none required for Phase 1–3:\n   662\t\n   663\t- **Lexical addressing**: `:var` gains `:yin.code/depth`/`:yin.code/index`\n   664\t  when the linearizer proves the name lexically bound; frames become vectors;\n   665\t  only unbound names walk store → primitives → modules.\n   666\t- **Superinstructions**: `:push-const`, `:push-var` fused by the loader (not\n   667\t  the linearizer, so datoms stay minimal).\n   668\t- **Host-array operand stack** inside the hot loop, materialized to a vector\n   669\t  only at park points (\"immutability observable only at park points\",\n   670\t  streams note §3).\n   671\t- **Content-addressed segments** (`:yin.code/hash`) for dedup across VMs and\n   672\t  for verifying a shipped continuation against its code.\n   673\t\n   674\tPhase 4 measured it: see §8.\n   675\t\n   676\t## §7. The Universal Continuation Format\n   677\t\n   678\tThe Semantic VM's linear CESK state represents the canonical, architecture-agnostic continuation format for the `datom.world` ecosystem. \n   679\t\n   680\tBecause execution state is modeled as pure data rather than host memory (Axiom 4), the linear bytecode state serves as the universal *lingua franca* for network-transparent continuations. The lowered representation (`{:segment id, :pc n, :env E, :stack S, :k K}`) resolves all execution-order ambiguity inherent in the Universal AST while remaining entirely decoupled from any specific hardware architecture.\n   681\t\n   682\t### 7.1 Cross-Architecture Normalization\n   683\tIf a highly specialized execution engine (e.g., a native WebAssembly compiler, a raw Register VM, or a hardware FPGA implementation) wishes to participate in the network, it must treat the Semantic VM state as the **normalized exchange format**:\n   684\t- **On Park (De-optimization):** When the specialized VM hits a blocking IO operation or a `:vm/park` instruction, it must map its specialized hardware registers or host stack back to the normalized linear format using the `:yin.code/source` mappings. It emits this standard Semantic VM continuation onto the stream.\n   685\t- **On Resume (Re-optimization):** When receiving a continuation off the network, the specialized VM reads the normalized `:pc` and `:stack`, lowers them into its own specialized hardware state, and resumes execution.\n   686\t\n   687\tBy enforcing the linear bytecode state as the universal standard, the network only ever trades simple integer PCs and array-based operand stacks. Any machine can pause execution on one continent, transmit the canonical state over a WebSocket, and resume execution on a radically different hardware architecture without losing semantic fidelity or performance.\n   688\t\n   689\t## §7. The Universal Continuation Format (Proposed)\n   690\t\n   691\t> [!WARNING]  \n   692\t> **Status: Proposed / Deferred.** The Semantic VM's linear CESK state is theoretically sound as an architecture-agnostic continuation format, but true heterogeneous network migration requires addressing several critical defects identified in the 2026-09-14 architectural review.\n   693\t\n   694\tThe Semantic VM's linear CESK state (`{:segment id, :pc n, :env E, :stack S, :k K}`) is proposed as the canonical exchange format for network-transparent continuations. Because this lowered representation resolves execution-order ambiguity inherent in the Universal AST, it provides a simpler target for specialized execution engines (e.g., WebAssembly, LLVM, hardware FPGA) to participate in the `datom.world` ecosystem.\n   695\t\n   696\tTo safely \"lift on park\" and \"lower on resume\" across heterogeneous boundaries without violating host isolation or concurrency invariants, the following contracts must be fully specified before this feature is accepted:\n   697\t\n   698\t1. **Safepoint Reconstruction Metadata:** `:yin.code/source` mappings are insufficient for state reconstruction. The compiler must generate exact safepoint metadata tying canonical PC locations to physical register/stack mappings so that arbitrary hardware states can be predictably lifted into the canonical CESK format.\n   699\t2. **Recursive Portable Encoding:** Section 1.1 permits host functions and local stream handles in the environment. A recursive serialization protocol is required to encode these local references into portable descriptors, with explicit failure modes for un-serializable resources.\n   700\t3. **Dependency Closure & Context:** A parked fragment is not a complete configuration. The format must explicitly declare its required primitives, loaded modules, and the global store state required for valid resumption.\n   701\t4. **Ownership Arbitration:** Emitting a continuation does not transfer ownership. The transport composition must define explicit arbitration events to prevent source-wakeup races and duplicate resumes by multiple readers.\n   702\t5. **Code Identity (Content Addressing):** Because tempids (`:segment 123`) are local to a single machine's database, continuations must refer to code via immutable, versioned semantic profiles (e.g., content hashing) to guarantee the receiving interpreter executes identical logic.\n   703\t\n   704\t## §8. Phase 4 Benchmarks\n   705\t\n   706\tMeasured 2026-09-14 on the development machine (macOS, OpenJDK 21.0.2).\n   707\t\n   708\t**Workload.** The historic tail-recursive countdown from the deleted\n   709\t`yin.vm.bytecode-bench`, unchanged, so the numbers read against the table in\n   710\t`docs/cesk-space-optimization.md`. **Timed region.** `vm/run` alone: VM\n   711\tconstruction, AST → datoms, lowering, and the program load are outside it,\n   712\tand every timed call runs the same loaded VM value. **Harness.**\n   713\t`test/bench/yin_vm_bench.cljc` (Criterium `quick-bench`; it checks the\n   714\tresult is 0 before timing).\n   715\t\n   716\t### 8.1 JVM\n   717\t\n   718\t| Mean time per run | n=500 | n=5000 | Ratio to walker (n=5000) |\n   719\t|---|---|---|---|\n   720\t| AST Walker (v2) | 8.64 ms | 86.96 ms | 1x |\n   721\t| **Semantic (v2)** | **1.04 ms** | **10.02 ms** | **0.115x** |\n   722\t\n   723\tCriterium's standard deviation was under 3% of the mean in every cell. Both\n   724\tevaluators scale linearly over the 10x (walker 10.1x, semantic 9.7x).\n   725\t\n   726\t**Against the target line.** The historic register and stack VMs are deleted,\n   727\tso the like-for-like reading is the ratio to the walker measured in the same\n   728\trun. The historic register VM ran at 1/1.32 = 0.76x the walker and stack at\n   729\t0.80x; the semantic VM runs at 0.115x, about 6.6x under that line rather than\n   730\tat it. Absolute times are not comparable across the two tables: this v2\n   731\twalker measures 86.96 ms against the historic v1 walker's 29.33 ms on a\n   732\tdifferent run, and the gap reflects the machine and v2's walker itself, not\n   733\tthe semantic VM. The target is met with margin.\n   734\t\n   735\t### 8.2 Node.js and Dart\n   736\t\n   737\tBoth harnesses exist and compile, but neither was run in this session\n   738\t(executing `node` and `dart` was not permitted), so this section reports no\n   739\tnumbers and asserts nothing about V8 or Dart:\n   740\t\n   741\t```sh\n   742\tclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile vm-bench   # compiles\n   743\tnode target/vm-bench.js 500 5000\n   744\t\n   745\tclj -M:cljd compile yin.register-bench-cljd-v2             # compiles\n   746\tdart run bin/register_bench_cljd_v2.dart                   # both evaluators, n=50000\n   747\t```\n   748\t\n   749\t§6.3 predicts the gap widens on those hosts, since keyword `case` and map\n   750\tlookups cost more there and the linear machine removes both from the hot\n   751\tpath. That prediction stays unverified until these two commands run.\n   752\t\n   753\t### 8.3 Default decision\n   754\t\n   755\tThe semantic VM meets the performance line on the JVM, so `yin.repl.core`\n   756\tnow defaults to `:semantic`; `(vm :ast-walker)` still selects the walker.\n   757\tBehavioural parity for the switch rests on the Phase 1–3 suites and on a\n   758\tREPL corpus that produced identical output under both evaluators. The corpus\n   759\tcovered arithmetic, `def`/`defn`, `*1` history, 10⁵ tail calls, 2·10⁴-deep\n   760\tnon-tail recursion, streams, Python and PHP input, datom literals, and error\n   761\ttext. Where the two still differ is recorded in\n   762\t[`yin.vm.divergence-register.md`](./yin.vm.divergence-register.md#the-semantic-vm-against-the-ast-walker).\n   308\t`yin.vm.parity-test`, against values produced by v1 in the same run rather\n   309\tthan against values chosen freshly.\n   310\t\n   311\t## The semantic VM against the ast-walker\n   312\t\n   313\tDeliverable of Phase 4 of [`yin.vm.semantic.md`](./yin.vm.semantic.md).\n   314\tBoth evaluators run on `yin.vm`, share `engine`, `ffi`, `module` and the\n   315\tobserver, and are measured against each other in §8 there. Everything above\n   316\tthis section applies to both. This section lists only where they differ.\n   317\t\n   318\t**Program values: same.** A REPL corpus produced byte-identical output under\n   319\tboth evaluators. It covered arithmetic, `def`/`defn`, `*1` history, `let`,\n   320\t`loop`/`recur`, 10⁵ tail calls, 2·10⁴-deep non-tail recursion, stream\n   321\tmake/put/cursor/next, Python and PHP input, datom literals, `compile`, and\n   322\tthe error text for an unbound symbol, a non-function, division by zero, and\n   323\tan undefined macro. The differences below are in what a composition or an\n   324\tinspector can observe, not in the values programs compute.\n   325\t\n   326\t1. **`eval` refuses an AST.** The walker's `vm/eval` converts, loads, and\n   327\t   runs an AST. The semantic VM executes only `:yin.code/*` segments and\n   328\t   throws on a non-nil AST. Lowering (`yin.vm.linearize`) belongs to the\n   329\t   composition: `linearize/ast-loader` wraps `vm-load-program` at the\n   330\t   observer boundary, and `yin.repl.core/eval-ast` sends semantic-VM\n   331\t   input through the program medium rather than calling `eval`.\n   332\t2. **Closures print differently.** A walker closure carries its AST\n   333\t   (`{:type :closure :params :body :env}`). A semantic closure carries a code\n   334\t   address (`{:type :closure :params :entry :segment :env}`). `(fn [x] x)`\n   335\t   at the REPL renders the two shapes. The shapes are not interchangeable:\n   336\t   a closure from one evaluator cannot be applied by the other.\n   337\t3. **Machine state has a different shape.** `vm/control` is\n   338\t   `{:segment id :pc n}` rather than an AST node. The operand stack is a\n   339\t   `:stack` register. `vm/continuation` is a vector of return frames\n   340\t   (innermost last), not a linked frame list. Parked and wait-set entries,\n   341\t   and reified continuations, carry `{segment pc env stack k}`. A\n   342\t   continuation from one evaluator cannot resume on the other.\n   343\t4. **`step` is one instruction.** The walker steps one AST transition. The\n   344\t   semantic VM steps one instruction of the lowered segment (`step` is the\n   345\t   hot loop with a fuel of one). Step counts and intermediate states\n   346\t   therefore differ for the same program. Final values do not.\n   347\t5. **Unsupported nodes fail at lowering, not at run.** `:yin/macro-expand`\n   348\t   and `:vm/store-update` are outside both corpora. The walker in v2 reports\n   349\t   `Unknown AST node type` when it reaches the node. The linearizer throws\n   350\t   `Cannot lower unsupported node <type>` before any instruction runs, so a\n   351\t   program containing such a node executes none of its prefix. No v2 surface\n   352\t   compiler emits `:vm/store-update`, so only hand-built ASTs can observe\n   353\t   this.\n   354\t6. **Segment identity is load-checked.** Loading different code under a\n   355\t   segment id already in `:code` is a load error; an identical reload is\n   356\t   accepted. The walker has no code table and no such conflict.\n   357\t   `ast-loader` lowers each batch below every loaded segment, so REPL\n   358\t   batches never collide.\n   359\t7. **Continuation handoff is code plus registers.** The semantic handoff\n   360\t   demo (`datomworld.demo.continuation-handoff-v2`) ships the segment's code\n   361\t   datoms with the EDN-encoded registers. A continuation that holds a live\n   362\t   stream resource is refused for shipment rather than shipped with a\n   363\t   dangling handle.\n   364\t\n   365\t**Streams and FFI: no divergence found.** Both evaluators dispatch effects\n   366\tthrough `engine/handle-effect` over the same outcome tables above. The\n   367\tretention divergence, gap handling, once-only FFI bridge, the call-pair check\n   368\tbefore parking, and correlation checking apply to both unchanged.\n   369\t`semantic_ffi_test`, `semantic_engine_test`, and\n   370\t`semantic_stream_observer_test` mirror the walker's suites on the semantic\n   371\tVM. The semantic VM's `:request-sent` wait entry is how a retained\n   372\tfull-buffer request becomes a response reader. It is a representation of the\n   373\twalker's behaviour, not a different behaviour.\n   374\t\n   375\t**Shared, not a divergence:** neither `reset` restores `:env`. Each keeps the\n   376\tenvironment the last run ended in.\n   430\t    (= :semantic (:vm-type state)) (eval-datoms state (vm/ast->datoms ast))\n   431\t    :else (let [state' (inject-last-value state)]\n   432\t            (finalize-eval state state' (vm/eval (:vm state') ast)))))\n   433\t\n   434\t\n   435\t(defn- eval-datoms\n   436\t  \"Evaluate one datom-literal program by appending it through the program\n   437\t   medium's writer and then driving observer coordination, which loads and\n   438\t   runs every observed batch.\n   439\t\n   440\t   The observer recovers across a gap on its own, but the shell reads the gap\n   441\t   count around the round: an increase means one or more batches were never\n   442\t   run, so resuming as though execution were complete would report a result\n   443\t   built on programs the VM never saw.  The loss is reported and the shell\n   444\t   refuses further evaluation until `(reset)`.\"\n   445\t  [state datoms]\n   446\t  (if (:ingress-loss? state)\n   447\t    [state (str \"Error: \" ingress-loss-text)]\n   448\t    (let [state' (inject-last-value state)\n   449\t          append (stream/append! (:program-stream state') (vec datoms))]\n   450\t      (if-not (= :dao.stream/ok (:dao.stream/outcome append))\n   451\t        [state (str \"Error: datom batch not ingested: \"\n   452\t                    (name (:dao.stream/outcome append)))]\n   453\t        (let [observer0 (:observer state')\n   454\t              gaps-before (:ingress-gaps observer0 0)\n   455\t              {:keys [observer] vm :consumer}\n   456\t              (observer/run-on-stream {:observer observer0,\n   457\t                                       :consumer (:vm state')}\n   458\t                                      engine/ready-for-ingress?\n   459\t                                      (:load-program state')\n   460\t                                      run-vm)\n   461\t              state'' (assoc state' :observer observer :vm vm)]\n   462\t          (cond\n   463\t            (> (:ingress-gaps observer 0) gaps-before)\n   464\t            [(assoc state'' :ingress-loss? true)\n   465\t             (str \"Error: \" ingress-loss-text)]\n   466\t\n   467\t            (vm/halted? vm)\n   468\t            (finalize-eval state state'' vm)\n   469\t\n   470\t            :else\n   471\t            (throw\n   472\t              (ex-info\n   473\t                \"Datom stream did not form a complete, runnable Yin VM program.\n   474\tHint: If you wanted to evaluate these datoms as data, use a quote: '[[...]]\"\n   475\t                {:root-id (:root-id (vm/index-datoms datoms))\n   476\t                 :datom-count (count datoms)}))))))))\n   477\t\n   478\t\n   479\t(defn- compile-clojure-forms\n   480\t  [forms]\n   481\t  (if (= 1 (count forms))\n   482\t    (yang.clojure/compile (first forms))\n   483\t    (yang.clojure/compile-program forms)))\n   484\t\n   485\t\n   486\t(defn- compile-source\n   487\t  [lang input]\n   535\t                         \"; supported: \" (pr-str (vec (keys vm-constructors))))]))\n   536\t      lang (let [lang (first args)]\n   537\t             (if (contains? lang-labels lang)\n   538\t               [(assoc state :lang lang) (str \"Switched to \" (get lang-labels lang))]\n   539\t               [state (str \"Error: Unknown Yin REPL language \" (pr-str lang)\n   540\t                           \"; supported: \" (pr-str (vec (keys lang-labels))))]))\n   541\t      compile [state (render-compile-output (compile-command-ast state (first args)))]\n   542\t      reset (let [{:keys [program-stream observer vm load-program]}\n   543\t                  (make-session (:vm-type state) (:output-stream state))]\n   544\t              [(assoc state\n   545\t                      :vm vm\n   546\t                      :load-program load-program\n   547\t                      :program-stream program-stream\n   548\t                      :observer observer\n   549\t                      :ingress-loss? false)\n   550\t               (str (get vm-labels (:vm-type state)) \" reset\")])\n   551\t      help [state help-text]\n   552\t      repl-state [state (format-value (repl-state state))]\n   553\t      quit [(assoc state :running? false) \"Bye\"]\n   554\t      telemetry [state telemetry-text]\n   555\t      [state (str \"Error: Unknown Yin REPL command \" (pr-str command))])))\n   556\t\n   557\t\n   558\t(defn- eval-parsed\n   559\t  [state trimmed parsed]\n   560\t  (try\n   561\t    (let [forms (:forms parsed)\n   562\t          form (when (= 1 (count forms)) (first forms))]\n   563\t      (cond\n   564\t        (and form (command-form? form)) (handle-command state form)\n   565\t        (and form (datom-stream? form)) (eval-datoms state form)\n   566\t        (and form (ast-map? form)) (eval-ast state form)\n   567\t        forms (if (= :clojure (:lang state))\n   568\t                (eval-ast state (compile-clojure-forms forms))\n   569\t                (eval-ast state (compile-source (:lang state) trimmed)))\n   570\t        (= :clojure (:lang state)) [state (format-error (:error parsed))]\n   571\t        :else (eval-ast state (compile-source (:lang state) trimmed))))\n   572\t    (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Exception :cljs js/Error) error\n   573\t      (let [[state' output-text] (drain-output state)]\n   574\t        [state' (str output-text (format-error error))]))))\n   575\t\n   576\t\n   577\t(defn eval-input\n   578\t  \"Evaluate one input line locally, returning `[state text]`.\n   579\t\n   580\t   Input whose brackets do not balance is retained as `:pending-input` and\n        (engine/handle-effect (put-registers vm seg pc val St E K)\n                              effect\n                              (when park-entry-fns\n                                {:park-entry-fns park-entry-fns}))]\n    (if blocked?\n      {:stop (assoc state :control nil :k nil)}\n      {:continue [state value]})))\n\n\n(defn- apply-call\n  \"The §4.2 call transition, with the arguments already isolated from the\n   operand stack. Returns `{:goto [seg pc val St E K vm image]}` to continue,\n   `{:stop vm}` when the call blocked, or throws for a non-function.\n\n   A tail call grows neither K nor St: the frame the caller would have pushed\n   is the frame its own caller gave it. A primitive that yields an effect\n   parks with the continuation after the call site.\"\n  [code seg pc val St E K vm tail? f args]\n  (cond\n    (= :closure (:type f))\n    (let [E' (merge (:env f) (zipmap (:params f) args))\n          frame {:type :return, :segment seg, :pc (inc pc), :env E,\n                 :stack-base (count St)}\n          seg' (:segment f)]\n      {:goto [seg' (:entry f) val St E' (if tail? K (conj K frame))\n              vm (get code seg')]})\n    (fn? f)\n    (let [result (apply f args)]\n      (if (module/effect? result)\n        (let [{:keys [state value blocked?]}\n              (engine/handle-effect\n                (put-registers vm seg pc val St E K)\n                result\n                {:park-entry-fns (call-park-entries seg pc E St K)})]\n          (if blocked?\n            {:stop (assoc state :control nil :k nil)}\n            {:goto [seg (inc pc) value St E K state (get code seg)]}))\n        {:goto [seg (inc pc) result St E K vm (get code seg)]}))\n    :else (throw (ex-info \"Cannot apply non-function\" {:fn f}))))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; The hot loop\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn- vm-hot\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc:12:   `:tailcall`, and the dispatch below switches on the integers.\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc:17:   `{:type :return :segment :pc :env :stack-base}` for calls, and the wait\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc:201:          frame {:type :return, :segment seg, :pc (inc pc), :env E,\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc:280:              ;; :return — pop a frame, or halt on an empty K (6, :return)\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc:348:              ;; :call with :yin.code/tail? — same, frame-free (20, :tailcall)\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc:349:              20 (let [argc (nth inst 1)\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc:569:                               [(:tailcall vm/opcode-table) (:yin.code/argc ia)]\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc:571:                       :return [opcode]\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc:668:    (engine/restore-initial-env initial-env res)))\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:92:(defn restore-initial-env\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
